<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colebrooke–Cameron Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colebrooke–Cameron Commission was a British royal commission sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> in 1829, led by William Colebrooke and Charles Cameron. Its reforms of 1833 unified the administration of the island, created executive and legislative councils, abolished compulsory labour service and encouraged English education and free trade.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the British conquest of the Kandyan kingdom in 1815 the whole island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> became a British colony. By 1828 government spending had far outrun revenue, in part because the island was run as two separate parts, the upcountry and the low country, and because of the cost of salaries and facilities for officers sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The British government had to meet the deficit itself, and the Colonial Secretary sent a Royal Commission of Eastern Inquiry to examine the administration and propose administrative, financial, economic and judicial reforms.</p><p>According to Sir Charles Jeffries, by the time the commission reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> most of its members had fallen away, leaving Major William Colebrooke, later Sir William. Charles Hay Cameron, the other commissioner, was in charge of investigating the judicial system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The commission's proposals of 1833 were radical for their time, and only some of them were adopted. They called for an Executive Council and a Legislative Council, the amalgamation of the Kandyan and Maritime provinces under a single government by the Governor in Council, the admission of Ceylonese to the Ceylon Civil Service and the abolition of rajakariya, the compulsory personal service owed in the Kandyan provinces. They also proposed a commission to manage education and a principal public school on the British model for English education and teacher training, which became the Colombo Academy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The reforms gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> its first form of constitutional government and of modern central administration, the first steps in modernising its economic system, and the beginnings of a uniform system of justice, education and civil service. The two councils later became the foundation of a representative legislature, education passed from the church to the government, and rajakariya was abolished soon after.</p><p>The constitution also met protest. European planters in the upcountry, who wanted the Legislative Council to fund roads and posts near their estates, objected that its three unofficial members were outnumbered by officials and that it had too little power, and in 1856 they formed the Planters' Association of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colebrooke–Cameron Commission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Colebrooke-Cameron</b> reforms of 1833 centralised administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-ceylon">British Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
